WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.892 --> 00:00:21.610
[MUSIC - ALABAMA SHAKES, "DON'T
 WANNA FIGHT NO MORE"]

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.500
NEIL PALMER: Good.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:26.000
Ready?

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:26.500
OK.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:27.750
Good afternoon, everybody.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.460
Thank you for coming.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.710
My name's Neil Palmer.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:35.000
I'm the CTO for the FIS
advanced technology group.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.650
Today, we're here to
talk to you about this.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.880
It's a very long winded
title, I do realize.

00:00:42.797 --> 00:00:44.380
We wanted to call
it, "How we're going

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.010
to save Western
civilization," and all

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.030
I got was this corporate
branded t-shirt.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:50.620
But that wouldn't
fly with marketing.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.070
So you got that.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.220
What we are going to talk
to you about today is

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.890
how we are planning to ingest
every piece of stock market

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.620
data from the US
markets, process them

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.900
in four hours on a
daily basis-- that's

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.170
15 terabytes-- I make
it available for market

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:11.630
reconstruction and
analysis to the regulators.

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:13.160
And so to help me
with that today,

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.495
and we'll demo a little bit
of what we've been building.

00:01:15.495 --> 00:01:19.630
I have Mr. Carter Page from
Google, a lead for Bigtable,

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.870
and Mr. Todd Ricker,
principal engineer from FIS,

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:25.340
who's going to be
driving the demo.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:29.860
Little bit about us, FIS
is the largest technology

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.140
financial firm, financial
technology firm, in the world.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:38.574
We build everything from online
banking systems to ATM software

00:01:38.574 --> 00:01:40.240
to help you get your
cash out, for those

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.510
who still use that,
through to asset management

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:46.840
software, trading
systems risk, everything

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.050
at the back end of Wall Street.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.770
My world's a little
bit different.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:55.500
My world is to go out and
do work in other industry

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.540
verticals, to look at
media, to look at internet

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:01.730
and to figure out what's going
on in emerging technologies

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.630
in those industries.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.630
We find that those
industries are typically

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:08.620
about 18 months ahead of the
financial services firms.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.590
And so doing real
work there allows

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:13.890
us to bring that knowledge in
house and apply to our products

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:16.619
and to our clients.

00:02:16.619 --> 00:02:18.160
So the problem we're
looking at today

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.620
is-- what on earth is
going on in Wall Street?

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:24.265
As you may have noticed in
the media over the last couple

00:02:24.265 --> 00:02:27.166
of years, there's been
some issues with the well

00:02:27.166 --> 00:02:28.290
functioning of Wall Street.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:31.830
And you look at the film,
"The Big Short", you

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:35.280
look at the book, "Flash Boys".

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.030
There's some
serious issues going

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:40.450
on in terms of the systems
and how they're being managed.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.810
And I think part of that
is because a lot of people

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.960
have forgotten that up
until about 15 years ago,

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.720
Wall Street drove significant
technology innovation.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.650
The acceleration,
the electronification

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.300
of the markets, the
straight-through processing

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.800
piece was all a competitive
advantage to them

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.090
and made them a lot of money.

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:01.940
The problem with
that is it's left us

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:05.310
with a large number of very
disparate systems on very

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:09.310
disparate technologies across
ever increasing numbers

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:12.560
of broker dealers and
execution environments.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.630
And so when you couple
what is essentially

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:19.360
technical debt with what could
be viewed as regulatory debt,

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.242
you have a much
greater risk profile

00:03:23.242 --> 00:03:25.200
to the well ordered
functioning of the markets.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.550
And given the way our
economy and our way of life

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.720
is based on the well order
functioning of those markets,

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:33.620
this is not a good thing.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:37.340
And so there is a need for
much better forensics in terms

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.060
of how the market's behaving,
and so that when things do

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:44.890
go wrong, we can actually
figure out what has happened.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:47.600
So I want to touch a little
bit about where we've

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.530
come from before we get to
what we're going to talk about,

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:53.730
which is the
consolidated audit trail.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.430
It was only about 40
years ago, we still

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:59.050
had pit trading, paper
trading, like you

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.120
see in all the old movies.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.610
And of the space
of 40 years, we've

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:07.400
gone from that through to
the high frequency trading

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.030
that you hear so
much about these days

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:14.010
where essentially 75%
of the trades placed

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:17.180
are algorithmically driven.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:19.790
This has greatly
increased data volumes,

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:23.220
and it's greatly
increased data complexity.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:27.860
And so it's gotten to the point
where many of the Wall Street

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.330
firms have embraced this
electronification quite

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:31.610
aggressively.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.960
It's not necessarily a
bad thing in of itself.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.220
It's driven a lot greater
price efficiencies.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:39.760
It's actually done a better
job for a lot of even

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.010
retail participants there.

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:43.910
But when things
do go wrong, they

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:47.380
create anomalies in the market,
which creates unfairness.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.060
And people suffer as a
result of the consequence.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:53.450
And so the goal is
that we need to have

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:56.210
a single system these
days that allows people

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:59.550
to understand what happened.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:04.370
And so you can see here in the
last not even 15, 20 years,

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:10.260
there's been astronomical data
growth in the financial market.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.540
It's interesting because
even though there's

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.950
been astronomical growth,
it's not uniform growth.

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:17.430
It's not one way
traffic, if you will.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.380
You can look at the
spike on this graph

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.140
and see 2008 volumes
reach peak, and then they

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:27.050
plunge as a result of the
recession on the bubble that

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:29.140
popped then.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:32.380
Also what happened
was that in 2010,

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:34.969
there was what's known
as the Flash Crash.

00:05:34.969 --> 00:05:37.260
And this was a problem with
the markets and the trading

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:41.030
where several trillion dollars
was wiped off the US markets

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:44.670
in a matter of minutes and
then mysteriously reappeared

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.620
about 30 minutes later.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:50.540
Now, I don't know about you, but
having several trillion dollars

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:54.360
magically disappear and then
reappear is probably not really

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:57.650
a good thing for
life in general.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:02.850
And so the SEC as a
result put together

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.155
a proposal to create the
consolidated audit trail,

00:06:06.155 --> 00:06:07.560
or CAT as it's known.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.350
We have our own very
special design CAT symbol.

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:12.160
Not just your usual
grumpy cat stuff.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.710
Thank you very much.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:17.730
And so really,
the goal of CAT is

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:22.360
to track every life cycle
event, every tick, every trade,

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:25.020
every piece of data that's
involved in the US market

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:27.180
in one place.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:29.610
Previously, the quote
has been that regulators

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:32.300
have been trying to use
bikes to catch Ferraris.

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:35.970
And so our goal is to build
the next generation system that

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:39.150
will allow them to understand
in a reasonable amount of time

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:42.070
what is happening in the market.

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:44.280
And so this is a
lot of data, right?

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:47.310
This is taking data
from all these silos

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:50.380
across all these banks, these
broker dealers, the executions,

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:55.000
the dark pools, and bringing
them into a single system.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:57.910
We're going to have to
ingest 100 billion market

00:06:57.910 --> 00:06:59.600
events per day.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:02.700
We have to process
them in four hours.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:06.270
That's 15 terabytes
a day for six years.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.160
That's 30 petabytes of data.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:10.430
That, and here's
the kicker-- we have

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:14.550
to be able to query
the whole 30 petabytes.

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:18.380
We are one of three final
bidders for this process.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:20.100
The process started
four years ago

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:22.080
with over 30 firms involved.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:23.860
It's down to the final three.

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.360
With the help of
Google, we're going

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:27.270
to show you a little bit about
what we've built and managed

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:28.250
to achieve so far.

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:34.510
There's three core
problems we'll go through.

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:38.780
Just the sheer scale of
ingesting that much data,

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.630
the sheer scale of processing
that much data, and what

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:45.291
is frankly, a ridiculous
time frame-- the four hours.

00:07:45.291 --> 00:07:47.290
And then the final problem
is how do we actually

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:51.040
get any actionable information
out of all that data.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:53.710
There is absolutely zero
point in us throwing this

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:57.390
into a big black hole and we
can't do anything with it.

00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:00.170
Although, when the
original RFP came out--

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:04.050
it was about 250 pages long,
and there was two lines in it

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:06.905
about query- it says we need
batch and we need ad hoc.

00:08:06.905 --> 00:08:08.280
So we were like,
well, thank you.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.830
That's extremely helpful.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:14.885
So what we're going
to do now is we're

00:08:14.885 --> 00:08:17.010
going to walk you through--
we're going to kick off

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:19.750
a demo that's going to
come in several parts

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:22.820
around data
ingestion, processing.

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:24.680
We'll talk about the
linkage because we

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:26.410
have to be able to
link life cycle events

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:27.899
across multiple reporters.

00:08:27.899 --> 00:08:30.190
And then we'll show you some
of the visualization tools

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:33.817
we're creating to actually
interrogate the information.

00:08:33.817 --> 00:08:35.900
And so for that, I'm going
to hand it over to Todd

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:36.775
to kick off the demo.

00:08:36.775 --> 00:08:37.733
TODD RICKER: Thank you.

00:08:37.733 --> 00:08:38.450
Thank you, Neil.

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:41.860
Can we get my demo
screen up here?

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:42.360
Perfect.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:43.140
Thank you.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:46.556
So I'm going to just start
by kicking off the job,

00:08:46.556 --> 00:08:48.180
and then I'll tell
you what it's doing.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:52.450
You can see I'm about
to run a Maven command.

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.210
I'll say I'm doing a
two part demo here.

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:56.150
I'm running the data
processing piece first,

00:08:56.150 --> 00:08:57.750
which is a data flow job.

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:00.950
And then a little later,
we'll look at the data,

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:03.180
and we'll see how we
can visualize that data.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:05.340
So let's kick it off.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:07.710
Should be exciting.

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:10.750
It's executing a data flow
job, that Java code sitting

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:14.210
on my local laptop, it's
pushing it out into the cloud

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:15.100
right now.

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:18.800
It's just standard
Maven stuff going on.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:21.320
So what does the job do?

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:26.060
The job is going to process 25
million synthetic trade events

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:28.831
that I generated this
morning from real price data

00:09:28.831 --> 00:09:29.580
from this morning.

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:33.269
So at the end of the demo,
you can compare prices

00:09:33.269 --> 00:09:34.060
to the real prices.

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:35.310
You'll see they're same.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:36.440
But it is synthetic data.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:39.455
So the job kicked off.

00:09:39.455 --> 00:09:42.539
I'll Alt Tab over to the
Google Cloud console.

00:09:42.539 --> 00:09:43.580
We should see it running.

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:44.150
Very good.

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:50.260
So what the job's going to
do, take those 25 million

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:51.240
synthetic trade events.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.260
They're stored in
Google Cloud Storage.

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:54.910
They're FIX messages.

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:59.520
FIX is the standard protocol
by which exchanges and banks

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:03.290
communicate trade events.

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:06.650
So it's going to copy those
out of Google Cloud Storage.

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:09.276
It's going to insert
them in the Bigtable.

00:10:09.276 --> 00:10:11.150
Then we'll make another
run through that data

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:13.840
and create the links
between the trade events.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.810
So where they have
relationships,

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:18.060
we'll link them at that phase.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:21.850
That's the part that's
difficult at scale.

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:25.330
And then finally, we'll copy
all that data from Bigtable

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:27.700
into BigQuery so
that we can easily

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:30.672
visualize it and query it.

00:10:30.672 --> 00:10:33.160
So, Neil, why don't you
tell us more about linkage?

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:34.540
NEIL PALMER: OK.

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:36.570
So we're going to let
that keep ticking along.

00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:38.370
Fingers crossed
I haven't angered

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:41.930
the demo gods of anytime soon.

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:45.060
So what a lot of
people maybe don't know

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:47.790
is that when you order
stock, when you purchase

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:49.830
a stock online through
your broker dealer--

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:54.860
be it Fidelity, Charles Schwab,
your Cousin Vinny, whoever.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:57.670
If you've just cashed out in
some highly successful IPO

00:10:57.670 --> 00:10:59.910
and you need to reinvest
it into something

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.070
a bit more interesting
and you want

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:06.079
to buy 10,000 shares of Google,
for example, you click Submit.

00:11:06.079 --> 00:11:08.370
You get an order confirmation
number back straightaway,

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:11.580
but that is not how that
trade gets executed.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:14.110
That trade gets routed
you're a broker dealer,

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:17.020
and your broker
dealer is governed

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:19.110
by a couple of regulatory
things, one of which

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:20.090
is best execution.

00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:21.610
They have to make
sure that you get

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:24.350
the best price for that trade.

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:27.240
But after that, they have a
lot of options about how they

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:29.510
might want to fill the trades.

00:11:29.510 --> 00:11:31.680
They might route it
to multiple exchanges.

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:33.730
They might route it to
other broker dealers.

00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:35.410
They might fill it themselves.

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:41.160
They might send it to dark
pools, a wide range of areas

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:44.170
they can do that generate
multiple, multiple hops

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:45.590
in the trade.

00:11:45.590 --> 00:11:48.950
And in fact, most
trades these days

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:51.360
that are actually executed,
at the point of execution,

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:55.289
on Wall Street are typically
no more than 200 shares

00:11:55.289 --> 00:11:57.080
because they don't want
to move the market.

00:11:57.080 --> 00:12:00.360
They don't want to tip their
hand around who's buying what.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:03.040
And in fact, there was an
incident earlier this week

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:05.400
where somebody fat
fingered some Apple shares,

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:09.190
and you could see a huge spike
over several seconds in Apple

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:11.370
share prices because
somebody was trying to buy

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:14.620
70,000 shares instead of 70.

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:17.010
So what this does
is it generates

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:21.160
thousands and thousands of
life cycle events, billions.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:23.240
And we have to reconstruct them.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:27.150
We have to be able to connect
the order execution back

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:29.270
to the ultimul beneficiary
owner, the person who

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:31.820
ultimately pays for the trade.

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:37.250
Now, the problem is this is a
regulatory transaction system.

00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:37.840
All right?

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:39.180
This is not log file analysis.

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:41.630
We cannot drop one
of these events.

00:12:41.630 --> 00:12:44.110
We cannot make a mistake.

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:47.590
And so we're not allowed
to use pattern matching.

00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:50.100
We're not allowed
to use fuzzy logic.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:54.300
We have to take the trade
events and connect them

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:58.330
from point of execution, point
of fill, reconstruct the tree,

00:12:58.330 --> 00:13:00.810
all the way back up
to the original order.

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:02.180
And that's complex.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:04.850
It takes a huge amount
of processing power,

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:08.270
and we have to have very
flexible schemas in order

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:12.900
to be able to handle attributes
the may vary based on reporter,

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:18.200
may vary based on asset
cost, may vary based on time.

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:21.620
And so for that, we
chose Cloud Bigtable.

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:26.150
And to talk about Cloud
Bigtable, here's Carter.

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:27.611
CARTER PAGE: Thanks, Neil.

00:13:27.611 --> 00:13:28.960
Hi, everybody.

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:30.140
My name is Carter Page.

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:33.410
I'm the engineering
manager for Cloud BigTable.

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.990
I'm also the engineering
manager for Bigtable

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:39.410
internally at Google as well.

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:43.090
At Google, we've had our fair
share of big data problems.

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:46.920
With a audacious mission
to organize the world's

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:48.590
information, to
make it universally

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:53.310
accessible and useful,
we've had to come up

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:55.790
with a whole suite
of big data tools,

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:59.810
as you've heard for the
last couple of days.

00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:03.170
We developed these different
tools-- GFS, MapReduce,

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:04.820
Bigtable, Dremel.

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:07.660
And all these things start to
come together into our cloud

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:13.050
tools to allow Neil and the team
to build something like this.

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:16.390
And all these things came out
of real needs that we had.

00:14:16.390 --> 00:14:19.020
They were actual problems
we had at Google,

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:20.995
and we sat down and
figured out what

00:14:20.995 --> 00:14:24.780
do we need to engineer
to solve this problem.

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:26.910
So for instance,
very early on, we're

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:29.660
basically collecting a copy
of the entire worldwide web.

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:32.836
And we're trying to figure out
where do we store this thing?

00:14:32.836 --> 00:14:34.710
And you can either have
an application trying

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:36.880
to figure out how to
shard it across a bunch

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:39.370
of different machines,
which puts a lot of burden

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:41.980
on the product team,
or you can come up

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:45.240
with abstraction, an API, which
simplifies this actually very

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:47.870
complicated process underneath.

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:50.425
And we did that and became GFS.

00:14:50.425 --> 00:14:53.210
GFS was later
upgraded, essentially

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:56.320
replaced with Colossus in 2010.

00:14:56.320 --> 00:14:59.540
It also has a key value
API in front of it

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:01.639
that we know internally
as Blobstore.

00:15:01.639 --> 00:15:04.055
And externally, you would know
it as Google Cloud Storage.

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:09.735
As we were building
out these files

00:15:09.735 --> 00:15:11.430
and we have a place
to store them now,

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:13.300
but now we're getting
a lot of updates.

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:17.420
And while a file system
is great at doing blocks,

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:21.050
it's really bad at doing
small reads and writes.

00:15:21.050 --> 00:15:23.580
So you got a copy of the
web, and you're updating

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:26.040
the latest pages that came in.

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:28.160
If you have just a file
system like we had before,

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:31.410
we had to rebuild the
entire index from scratch.

00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:34.150
And this was taking
days, turn into weeks,

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:37.210
and was approaching a month
and it was creating a problem.

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:40.260
You had data staleness issues
in addition to the fact

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:44.330
that this was costing
more and more resources

00:15:44.330 --> 00:15:45.690
and time to actually run.

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:50.070
So we had to build a
database on petabyte scale,

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:53.180
and what we came up
with was Bigtable.

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:56.060
We launched an internal
service in 2006.

00:15:56.060 --> 00:15:58.970
So this was actually
our internal cloud.

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:01.910
Externally, we launched this
as Google Cloud Bigtable a year

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:04.980
ago, but this is actually
extracting the internal cloud

00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:08.460
service we've been
running for a decade.

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:09.880
So you have a whole
bunch of data,

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:12.940
and you want to be able to
do an ad hoc query on say,

00:16:12.940 --> 00:16:14.000
a trillion rows.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:17.590
And you want to be able to just
ask some basic questions of it.

00:16:17.590 --> 00:16:20.930
It's not a very easy thing to
do without something like what

00:16:20.930 --> 00:16:22.930
we developed internally,
Dremel, or if you

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:25.800
saw the previous presentation,
BigQuery, which is also

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:26.800
talked about a lot here.

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:31.534
And there's a fun kiosk
on BigQuery out there.

00:16:31.534 --> 00:16:32.950
And then you've
got all this data.

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:34.616
You want to do something
with it, right?

00:16:34.616 --> 00:16:37.590
You want to identify which
photos have cats in them.

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:40.460
You want to figure out what
your page ranking is going

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:42.150
to be for your search index.

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:44.000
You want to do something
useful with it.

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:46.670
So you need to actually have
data processing at scale

00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:48.700
massively parallel processing.

00:16:48.700 --> 00:16:51.900
We built three tools
internally-- MapReduce, Flume,

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:53.110
and MillWheel.

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:55.560
And these things became
what you see up here,

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:56.990
which is data flow.

00:16:56.990 --> 00:16:59.500
Data flow is the
kind of conductor

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:03.300
that helps tie all
these things together.

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:07.420
It's a really key piece to be
able to do big data analytics.

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:09.300
You need something
that can actually

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:12.880
talk to Bigtable, for instance,
at the scale of a Bigtable

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:15.220
becomes useful.

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:17.460
So imagine what you
can build if you

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:22.720
have these products, these
services internally that just

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:24.040
will magically scale for you.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:26.780
Whether you have 10,000
customers or whether you

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:31.160
have 100 million customers,
you can think about the product

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:34.560
and not think about how
you're going to scale it up

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:36.050
at each step along
the way and how

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:38.920
you're going to deal with
sharding your databases.

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:40.510
When you don't have
to think about it,

00:17:40.510 --> 00:17:42.900
you can focus on building
really great products.

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:46.600
And I really think that we
contribute a lot of the product

00:17:46.600 --> 00:17:49.770
diversity that Google's
been able to develop over

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:54.150
the years to this really
rich infrastructure.

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:56.190
And this is the same
infrastructure that

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:57.490
is in Google Cloud Platform.

00:17:57.490 --> 00:18:00.200
And that's, I think, what
makes this really exciting

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:02.670
for a lot of us,
is the idea of this

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:05.650
was an enabling factor
for Google to be

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:08.290
able to grow and
have this diversity

00:18:08.290 --> 00:18:09.860
and richness of our products.

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:12.270
We think that external
customers will

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:14.616
be able to find new
and exciting ways to be

00:18:14.616 --> 00:18:15.990
able to do things
when they don't

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:18.800
have to worry about the basics.

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:21.220
So back to Cloud
Bigtable, which is, again,

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:22.790
my area of expertise.

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:24.950
So I'll spend a couple
of minutes on this.

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:27.410
It's the exact same
service in Cloud BigTable

00:18:27.410 --> 00:18:32.950
as Photos and Maps and
Calendar and et cetera talk to.

00:18:32.950 --> 00:18:35.634
It's not an abstraction or
a Bigtable light version

00:18:35.634 --> 00:18:36.300
we have running.

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:38.490
It's actually the
exact same service.

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:45.490
It just has an external cloud
API kind of attached to it.

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:47.600
Cloud Bigtable, for those
of you who don't know,

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:49.058
I'll give you a
quick introduction.

00:18:49.058 --> 00:18:50.370
It's a NoSQL database.

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:52.120
So it's not relational.

00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:55.000
You define columns
as you insert them.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:57.160
It supports sequential
scans, which basically

00:18:57.160 --> 00:18:59.430
lets you do some clever
things which are good for time

00:18:59.430 --> 00:19:01.080
series and the like.

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:03.310
And it'll autoadjust
access patterns.

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:05.980
So you've got this
large sharded database.

00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:08.050
And as you change
your access patterns,

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:10.580
it will adjust the
data for you so you

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:13.080
don't have to think about it.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:16.310
Briefly how it works is
you have a set of clients

00:19:16.310 --> 00:19:18.520
out there-- usually a
lot more than that-- that

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:20.670
are talking to a set of nodes.

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:24.120
Now, the nodes are
basically just CPU and RAM,

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:28.310
and they will pin to a certain
chunk of the actual data that

00:19:28.310 --> 00:19:32.360
sits in the Colossus file system
which handles our durability

00:19:32.360 --> 00:19:34.830
and basically replicates
that data for us.

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:37.210
And each node will serve
out a chunk of that data

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:39.450
to the clients.

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:41.960
What this allows us
to do is it allows--

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:46.570
in Cloud BIgtable-- allows you
to scale processing and file

00:19:46.570 --> 00:19:48.860
size orthogonality.

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:51.150
So for instance, if
you have a lot of data

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:52.650
but you're just
kind of trickling it

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:54.470
in over the course
of a few years,

00:19:54.470 --> 00:19:57.650
you could have a few
nodes and let that grow.

00:19:57.650 --> 00:20:00.180
Or if you're doing a lot
of really fast processing

00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:03.970
on a couple of
terabytes, you don't

00:20:03.970 --> 00:20:07.260
need to actually scale
your nodes to the same size

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:09.480
as your actual data.

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:12.860
So I was talking about
learning access patterns.

00:20:12.860 --> 00:20:15.630
For instance, imagine you have
a hypothetical situation where

00:20:15.630 --> 00:20:18.510
we've got a really small-- three
nodes is a very small cluster.

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:20.370
It's for production.

00:20:20.370 --> 00:20:23.110
But it's like the minimum size
we allow in Cloud Bigtable.

00:20:23.110 --> 00:20:25.960
But imagine for
illustration purposes,

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:27.490
we have a three node cluster.

00:20:27.490 --> 00:20:29.870
And by the luck of the
draw, all your clients

00:20:29.870 --> 00:20:32.060
are all asking for the
data that's sitting

00:20:32.060 --> 00:20:36.120
in the node on the far left.

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:40.010
What Bigtable will do is it will
identify these load patterns

00:20:40.010 --> 00:20:43.000
as they start to appear,
and it'll immediately

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:44.520
start shuffling data around.

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:46.720
It's actually really
fast at doing this.

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:50.060
It can move entire chunks of
data in a couple of seconds.

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:52.440
And if the access
patterns change again

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:54.670
and there's a new server
that starts to heat up,

00:20:54.670 --> 00:20:56.760
it'll also start to rebalance.

00:20:56.760 --> 00:21:00.630
And that's useful when
you start to think

00:21:00.630 --> 00:21:02.315
about how you want to scale it.

00:21:04.872 --> 00:21:06.330
Basically, what
you get out of this

00:21:06.330 --> 00:21:08.145
is you get linear scalability.

00:21:08.145 --> 00:21:10.960
You can go from three
nodes to 30 nodes,

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:13.840
and you get 10 X increase
in terms of your throughput.

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:17.550
You can go from for
30 nodes to 300 nodes.

00:21:17.550 --> 00:21:21.320
You get another 10 X. It
is linear all the way out.

00:21:21.320 --> 00:21:23.920
You go to the kind of
sizes that they'll need

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:26.285
to run-- it just keeps going.

00:21:26.285 --> 00:21:28.160
And that's actually
really great for planning

00:21:28.160 --> 00:21:31.305
because a lot of times these
[INAUDIBLE] architectures

00:21:31.305 --> 00:21:33.930
will eventually start to flatten
out a little bit on the curve.

00:21:33.930 --> 00:21:37.740
But you can actually count
on your line going straight,

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:39.280
and you don't have
to do a load test

00:21:39.280 --> 00:21:41.640
at an order of 1,000 nodes.

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:43.940
You can do it at
100 nodes and know

00:21:43.940 --> 00:21:46.960
what resources you're going
to need for that higher set.

00:21:46.960 --> 00:21:49.330
It also gives you confidence.

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:52.270
As you grow, you don't have
to worry about it too much.

00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:55.220
Your cost of your
underlying database

00:21:55.220 --> 00:21:58.840
can be just a function
of your growth.

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:01.450
So internally-- and
getting to the point

00:22:01.450 --> 00:22:03.950
where we exposed Cloud
Bigtable last year--

00:22:03.950 --> 00:22:06.320
it's a lot of engineering
that we did internally

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:08.910
to be able to have
this cloud product.

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:11.600
Again, it's essentially a
cloud product internally.

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:14.080
We had to teach Bigtable
how to configure itself.

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:16.470
It started with thousands
of different flags

00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:18.550
and configurations
that were really

00:22:18.550 --> 00:22:20.797
confusing to internal engineers.

00:22:20.797 --> 00:22:22.630
And we realized that
Bigtable could actually

00:22:22.630 --> 00:22:25.880
do a better job figuring out how
to configure itself correctly

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:27.870
than our engineers
could a lot of the time.

00:22:27.870 --> 00:22:31.040
And this results in a simpler
control plane on the cloud

00:22:31.040 --> 00:22:32.390
interface for it.

00:22:32.390 --> 00:22:33.690
We do a lot of isolation.

00:22:33.690 --> 00:22:36.720
We had to make sure that
one product at Google that

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:39.260
was having a very big
day was not interfering

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:41.270
with another product at Google.

00:22:41.270 --> 00:22:45.030
And as I showed before, there's
a rebalancing aspect to it

00:22:45.030 --> 00:22:47.130
that we've been spending
a lot of time on.

00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:49.870
And this is true of all these
products you're seeing up here.

00:22:49.870 --> 00:22:52.300
These aren't things that
were invented for cloud.

00:22:52.300 --> 00:22:54.800
These are things that we've
been developing and running

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:57.470
and using internally and are
instrumental to operations

00:22:57.470 --> 00:22:59.000
for the past decade.

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:00.700
And the same sort
of work we've had

00:23:00.700 --> 00:23:03.320
to do to be able to make
all these products--

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:05.140
so these Google
products co-exist side

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:09.470
by side-- that's actually what
puts us in a great position

00:23:09.470 --> 00:23:13.610
to have products that work
very well in a cloud scenario.

00:23:13.610 --> 00:23:16.820
So with that, I'll
hand it back to Neil.

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:18.350
NEIL PALMER: Thank
you very much.

00:23:18.350 --> 00:23:21.440
So what Carter's
just been describing

00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:25.400
was very important for us from
a design decision perspective.

00:23:25.400 --> 00:23:30.930
The levels of load we're talking
about-- with Google taking

00:23:30.930 --> 00:23:34.470
the technologies that we
already know scale that level,

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:36.170
if not beyond that level.

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:37.950
For me, that was a
weight off my mind.

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:40.005
Like I don't have
to worry about this.

00:23:40.005 --> 00:23:41.240
Is this going to scale?

00:23:41.240 --> 00:23:42.711
I know it's going to scale.

00:23:42.711 --> 00:23:44.960
So I'm going to talk a little
bit about the high level

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:45.510
architecture.

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:47.260
But first, I want to
check in on the demo.

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:48.340
Todd how is it going?

00:23:48.340 --> 00:23:49.840
TODD RICKER: It's
going pretty well.

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:55.470
We've ingested 26 million
order events, market events.

00:23:55.470 --> 00:23:59.540
We've linked them, and we're
finalizing with the BigQuery

00:23:59.540 --> 00:24:00.140
copy.

00:24:00.140 --> 00:24:00.806
It'll be a race.

00:24:00.806 --> 00:24:01.920
We'll see what happens.

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:02.670
NEIL PALMER: I'll slow down.

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:03.420
TODD RICKER: Yeah.

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:04.760
[LAUGHS]

00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:09.010
NEIL PALMER: So just a little
bit about the thought process

00:24:09.010 --> 00:24:10.990
when this started in 2012.

00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:14.970
And a lot's changed
in those four years.

00:24:14.970 --> 00:24:17.960
FIS runs a lot of infrastructure
and a lot of processes

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:22.450
and a lot of applications
for financial services firms.

00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:25.580
But we looked at the scale
of this, and we thought, no.

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:26.090
Too big.

00:24:26.090 --> 00:24:27.316
We can't handle that.

00:24:29.140 --> 00:24:31.290
We started to look
at private cloud

00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:35.280
as an option-- addresses
some of the scale issues,

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:37.860
definite economic issues.

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:40.650
And so we came to
the point where

00:24:40.650 --> 00:24:45.090
we're looking at public cloud,
and it became clear to us

00:24:45.090 --> 00:24:46.970
that they had the scale
and you could already

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:51.350
see the impact of the economics
and the impacts of Moore's law

00:24:51.350 --> 00:24:53.480
with prices decreasing.

00:24:53.480 --> 00:24:55.880
And so we decided that
was the way to go.

00:24:55.880 --> 00:24:58.720
I have to say at the time
in financial services, that

00:24:58.720 --> 00:25:02.040
was not a particularly
popular decision.

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:04.350
When we first walked in
front of the regulators

00:25:04.350 --> 00:25:06.330
with Google in the
room and told them

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:09.040
what we were planning on doing,
they basically were like,

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:12.120
you are out of your mind.

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:14.870
And so on one hand, the process
has taken a really long time

00:25:14.870 --> 00:25:17.737
because that's the way
regulatory systems work.

00:25:17.737 --> 00:25:19.570
On the other hand,
that's good because we've

00:25:19.570 --> 00:25:23.800
had the journey that's brought
the regulators along with us

00:25:23.800 --> 00:25:26.400
in terms of understanding
what public cloud is,

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:30.490
how the security works, and
especially the economics.

00:25:30.490 --> 00:25:34.140
And the continued price
reductions in all the public

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:36.640
cloud providers has made
a significant difference

00:25:36.640 --> 00:25:39.730
in terms of the risk
cost-benefit equation.

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:43.614
So in terms of high
level architecture,

00:25:43.614 --> 00:25:45.280
you've heard Todd
talk about some of it.

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:48.630
We have a direct pairing
connection with Google and My5

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:50.100
and the data center there.

00:25:50.100 --> 00:25:51.940
It's a 10 gig pipe.

00:25:51.940 --> 00:25:55.230
Right now, we're
pushing L2 equities data

00:25:55.230 --> 00:25:57.130
in through it on
a regular basis.

00:25:57.130 --> 00:25:59.440
We're only consuming about
500 megs of that bandwidth.

00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:01.995
We've got 20 times
headroom ready to go.

00:26:01.995 --> 00:26:02.495
It smokes.

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:05.590
We drop.

00:26:05.590 --> 00:26:09.420
It's basically fix, drop copy
the files into cloud storage.

00:26:09.420 --> 00:26:11.720
We use data flow
to transform and do

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:13.910
an initial validation
on the data

00:26:13.910 --> 00:26:16.190
and insert it into Bigtable.

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:18.030
We run another
job that then does

00:26:18.030 --> 00:26:21.200
the linkage on all
the events in Bigtable

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:24.980
before extracting
it out to BigQuery.

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:27.370
Bigtable was critical
because as I said before,

00:26:27.370 --> 00:26:29.490
we have to link
from the ground up.

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:31.920
And so when we get an
event-- unless it's

00:26:31.920 --> 00:26:36.470
an absolutely new order, unless
it's the very first step-- what

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:41.150
we know is we have the event
and we know its parent event.

00:26:41.150 --> 00:26:44.520
And so what we do with Bigtable
is essentially we write a put.

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:46.260
We do a put and put the row in.

00:26:46.260 --> 00:26:48.260
But we also do
essentially another put

00:26:48.260 --> 00:26:52.200
which puts a phantom
row in for the parent.

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:55.180
And so it allows us to know,
by constructing the column

00:26:55.180 --> 00:27:00.140
names on the fly, whether or
not the previous or subsequent

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:02.940
steps in the chain have
already been found.

00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:05.630
Part of the problem is we
have no guarantees on when

00:27:05.630 --> 00:27:08.140
we're going to see this data.

00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:11.520
The banks have to
report it by-- I believe

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:13.860
it's 8 PM end of day trading.

00:27:13.860 --> 00:27:15.560
We have four hours
to process it,

00:27:15.560 --> 00:27:17.229
but we don't know the orders.

00:27:17.229 --> 00:27:18.895
We don't know the
sequence of the orders

00:27:18.895 --> 00:27:21.190
and when they come
in, none of that.

00:27:21.190 --> 00:27:23.200
And so you have to be
able to very flexibly

00:27:23.200 --> 00:27:26.632
see whether or not the full
life cycle has been found.

00:27:26.632 --> 00:27:28.340
And there are some
orders that you won't.

00:27:28.340 --> 00:27:32.347
Some orders are
out until cancel.

00:27:32.347 --> 00:27:34.180
The other big thing
that was important to us

00:27:34.180 --> 00:27:37.874
was BigQuery and the
API access to BigQuery.

00:27:37.874 --> 00:27:39.290
I don't know about
you guys, but I

00:27:39.290 --> 00:27:42.280
hate getting requests
from business people

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:43.380
to write me a new report.

00:27:43.380 --> 00:27:44.379
That drives me practice.

00:27:48.030 --> 00:27:52.050
And because we have to bring
an entire industry with us--

00:27:52.050 --> 00:27:53.970
we have to move
an entire industry

00:27:53.970 --> 00:27:56.370
from a variety of different
regulatory systems

00:27:56.370 --> 00:27:58.960
onto this one, and
it's an industry

00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:01.510
that is basically saying
you're going to pry Excel

00:28:01.510 --> 00:28:04.490
from my cold, dead hands.

00:28:04.490 --> 00:28:08.980
So the fact that BigQuery means
we can plug Excel into it,

00:28:08.980 --> 00:28:12.410
we can plug other BI tools
into it without our involvement

00:28:12.410 --> 00:28:14.152
was very, very important.

00:28:14.152 --> 00:28:15.860
And so we'll talk a
little bit about that

00:28:15.860 --> 00:28:18.040
later on in some of
the visualizations that

00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:19.050
are going on.

00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:23.560
We haven't gotten
this far yet, but I

00:28:23.560 --> 00:28:26.710
think the next major
challenge for us

00:28:26.710 --> 00:28:30.330
is going to be around testing
tools and regression testing

00:28:30.330 --> 00:28:32.800
and things that we give the
industry so that they can

00:28:32.800 --> 00:28:35.260
understand how our
software is working,

00:28:35.260 --> 00:28:38.160
how our system is working,
how their order management

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:39.650
systems are operating.

00:28:39.650 --> 00:28:41.380
When they upgrade
their software,

00:28:41.380 --> 00:28:43.830
how do they know that
it's compatible with what

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:44.700
we've done lately?

00:28:44.700 --> 00:28:46.866
And I think that's going
to be a big, big challenge.

00:28:49.230 --> 00:28:49.940
Are we ready?

00:28:49.940 --> 00:28:51.682
Are we going to
look at the demo?

00:28:51.682 --> 00:28:52.390
TODD RICKER: Yes.

00:28:52.390 --> 00:28:53.460
NEIL PALMER: All right.

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:55.335
TODD RICKER: The demo
gods have smiled on me,

00:28:55.335 --> 00:28:57.900
and the job completed,
First I'm going

00:28:57.900 --> 00:29:02.480
to show you a BI tool
on another set of data.

00:29:02.480 --> 00:29:04.105
This is not the data
we just processed.

00:29:06.620 --> 00:29:08.620
This is a BI tool
called ClixSense.

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:09.850
It's a web based tool.

00:29:09.850 --> 00:29:12.010
It uses the BigQuery connector.

00:29:12.010 --> 00:29:16.460
So we can nicely connect
it to our data sources.

00:29:16.460 --> 00:29:20.030
As BI tools go, you can
set up the visualizations

00:29:20.030 --> 00:29:22.960
without necessarily
using a programmer.

00:29:22.960 --> 00:29:26.260
So business users can set
up their own visualizations,

00:29:26.260 --> 00:29:29.171
help them do their own job well.

00:29:29.171 --> 00:29:34.490
And this is powerful stuff in an
industry that's still primarily

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:36.220
run on Excel spreadsheets.

00:29:36.220 --> 00:29:40.960
So here, we're looking at the
most active underlying options

00:29:40.960 --> 00:29:43.256
from today-- from this morning.

00:29:43.256 --> 00:29:45.870
Market open until about 11.

00:29:45.870 --> 00:29:46.840
We have a pie chart.

00:29:46.840 --> 00:29:49.940
We have a bar chart,
pretty simple stuff.

00:29:49.940 --> 00:29:55.640
You can see that SPDR's ETF was
the most actively traded option

00:29:55.640 --> 00:29:56.700
this morning.

00:29:56.700 --> 00:29:59.690
That's typical of
SPDR as a ETF fund.

00:29:59.690 --> 00:30:01.810
That's tracking the S and P 500.

00:30:01.810 --> 00:30:05.750
So on most normal days, it's
going to be a very high volume.

00:30:05.750 --> 00:30:07.140
Little odometer.

00:30:07.140 --> 00:30:07.700
Great.

00:30:07.700 --> 00:30:10.470
Very simple, but powerful stuff.

00:30:10.470 --> 00:30:13.720
So let's move on and look at
another visualization that adds

00:30:13.720 --> 00:30:14.680
had some fun.

00:30:14.680 --> 00:30:16.995
So we have here a heat map.

00:30:16.995 --> 00:30:20.110
It's the same data source,
same underlying data.

00:30:20.110 --> 00:30:22.160
We've added the
dimension of the trading

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:23.890
venues of up at the top there.

00:30:23.890 --> 00:30:27.690
So you can see the
extra various exchanges.

00:30:27.690 --> 00:30:30.450
The darker color
denotes higher volume.

00:30:30.450 --> 00:30:34.000
So we can easily look
across all the exchanges

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:37.190
and see that SPDR's
on the ISE exchange.

00:30:37.190 --> 00:30:41.460
ISE exchange was the
highest volume this morning.

00:30:41.460 --> 00:30:43.180
So that's fine and good.

00:30:43.180 --> 00:30:46.160
Let's look at something
even more complex.

00:30:46.160 --> 00:30:50.640
This psychedelic looking
thing is the zoomable sunburst

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:51.630
visualization.

00:30:51.630 --> 00:30:53.510
I think that's pretty
arbitrarily named,

00:30:53.510 --> 00:30:57.240
but we can really drill
down and see even more data

00:30:57.240 --> 00:30:58.540
with this visualization.

00:30:58.540 --> 00:31:03.460
The inside wheel here is the
various highly traded options,

00:31:03.460 --> 00:31:04.260
underlying options.

00:31:04.260 --> 00:31:06.400
So we see SPDR here.

00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:08.100
We can drill in on
that, and then we

00:31:08.100 --> 00:31:13.060
see that middle wheel is
the various exchanges.

00:31:13.060 --> 00:31:16.720
So we can see that on
the ISE, SPDR's was

00:31:16.720 --> 00:31:17.990
at the highest volume there.

00:31:17.990 --> 00:31:19.560
And then we can
drill in even further

00:31:19.560 --> 00:31:24.710
and see that the majority
of the orders were puts.

00:31:24.710 --> 00:31:26.195
So that's great.

00:31:26.195 --> 00:31:27.150
It's BI tool.

00:31:27.150 --> 00:31:31.700
It doesn't necessarily
require programmers.

00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:35.880
The next thing we
built was a custom app.

00:31:35.880 --> 00:31:39.140
And this is going to visualize
the data we just processed.

00:31:39.140 --> 00:31:44.650
So my web app is
connected up to that data

00:31:44.650 --> 00:31:50.010
that we just processed,
and it is built with D3

00:31:50.010 --> 00:31:55.670
and also uses the
BigQuery data connector.

00:31:55.670 --> 00:31:58.390
So let's look at Google
stock for this morning.

00:32:04.800 --> 00:32:07.060
There we go.

00:32:07.060 --> 00:32:08.570
So we have a scatter plot here.

00:32:08.570 --> 00:32:11.900
These are all market events.

00:32:11.900 --> 00:32:14.000
This is my synthetic
market events,

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:16.870
but based on real pricing data
from this morning, from market

00:32:16.870 --> 00:32:18.820
open until about 11.

00:32:18.820 --> 00:32:22.410
The various colors are
different market event type.

00:32:22.410 --> 00:32:27.820
So these blue are
new order events.

00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:30.240
There's routing
events and a bunch

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:31.730
of different kind of events.

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:34.390
But let's look at one
of the filled events

00:32:34.390 --> 00:32:36.410
because they are the
interesting ones.

00:32:36.410 --> 00:32:38.810
They've reached the end
of their life cycle--

00:32:38.810 --> 00:32:41.991
or at least a
portion of them has.

00:32:41.991 --> 00:32:42.490
OK.

00:32:42.490 --> 00:32:47.170
So here is our D3
visualization of linkage.

00:32:47.170 --> 00:32:50.110
Neil walked us through
what linkage meant.

00:32:50.110 --> 00:32:55.080
This is a single order
and the life cycle

00:32:55.080 --> 00:32:56.590
it took on to be filled.

00:32:56.590 --> 00:33:01.876
So if we see the blue dot up
there, that was the new order.

00:33:01.876 --> 00:33:03.250
When I mouse over
at the top, you

00:33:03.250 --> 00:33:04.730
can see the specifics
of the order.

00:33:04.730 --> 00:33:07.230
So 458 shares.

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:11.610
It was a routed through a
couple different broker dealers.

00:33:11.610 --> 00:33:13.000
And then at this
point, the order

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:16.390
was split into six
smaller orders.

00:33:16.390 --> 00:33:18.830
So they branch out.

00:33:18.830 --> 00:33:23.220
We can see that what started as
a few hundred shares is now 91.

00:33:23.220 --> 00:33:26.270
So we're breaking down the
order into smaller bits.

00:33:26.270 --> 00:33:30.220
Got routed again, and then
finally it was filled.

00:33:30.220 --> 00:33:33.680
And the other path
is it goes out,

00:33:33.680 --> 00:33:36.140
gets split a bunch of times,
and it's not yet filled.

00:33:36.140 --> 00:33:41.020
So those smaller
orders are still open.

00:33:41.020 --> 00:33:43.400
So again, this is
D3 visualization

00:33:43.400 --> 00:33:44.850
using the BigQuery connector.

00:33:44.850 --> 00:33:47.083
It's all client side.

00:33:47.083 --> 00:33:50.450
It's deployed to App Engine,
but there's really no smarts

00:33:50.450 --> 00:33:52.280
on the app server side.

00:33:52.280 --> 00:33:55.020
It's all client side.

00:33:55.020 --> 00:33:56.840
So the final thing
I'm going to show you

00:33:56.840 --> 00:34:02.590
is the real Swiss army
knife of data processing.

00:34:02.590 --> 00:34:05.620
This is Google Cloud Datalab.

00:34:05.620 --> 00:34:06.770
It's a Python based tool.

00:34:06.770 --> 00:34:07.610
It's web based.

00:34:07.610 --> 00:34:11.050
It's built on IPython notebooks.

00:34:11.050 --> 00:34:14.210
I've hidden some of the code
here-- the rendering code--

00:34:14.210 --> 00:34:15.889
but it's not too much.

00:34:15.889 --> 00:34:17.440
I'm calling it there at the top.

00:34:17.440 --> 00:34:21.190
You can see the BigQuery query
that we're issuing there.

00:34:21.190 --> 00:34:22.770
It's pretty simple.

00:34:22.770 --> 00:34:26.330
And then what this graph is
doing-- the underlying data

00:34:26.330 --> 00:34:29.310
is the price data for
Bank of America on a day

00:34:29.310 --> 00:34:31.719
a couple months ago
back in November.

00:34:31.719 --> 00:34:35.090
So it's one day of price
data for Bank of America.

00:34:35.090 --> 00:34:36.860
The yellow line is
the moving average.

00:34:36.860 --> 00:34:39.030
You probably can barely
see the yellow line.

00:34:39.030 --> 00:34:41.000
But there's a moving
average line there.

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:44.870
And the red dots
indicate when the price

00:34:44.870 --> 00:34:47.830
has gotten 3 and 1/2 standard
deviations away from the moving

00:34:47.830 --> 00:34:48.330
average.

00:34:48.330 --> 00:34:52.670
So these are like anomalies
in the price during the day.

00:34:52.670 --> 00:34:56.584
So this is interesting
to traders and auditors

00:34:56.584 --> 00:34:57.500
and a bunch of people.

00:34:57.500 --> 00:35:00.070
So this is your
data scientist tool.

00:35:00.070 --> 00:35:02.580
People who can program,
people who know Python,

00:35:02.580 --> 00:35:05.330
you're able to use NumPy,
SciPy, Pandas-- a lot

00:35:05.330 --> 00:35:10.690
of the real powerful analytics
tools that we have available.

00:35:10.690 --> 00:35:14.280
So that's three ways
of looking at our data.

00:35:14.280 --> 00:35:15.020
Going to Neil.

00:35:15.020 --> 00:35:16.400
NEIL PALMER: Thank you.

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:18.210
So you kind of
get a feel there--

00:35:18.210 --> 00:35:21.540
tools for business users, tools
for day to day operations,

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:23.250
and then tools for
your data science guys

00:35:23.250 --> 00:35:26.500
to do the exploration.

00:35:26.500 --> 00:35:28.785
Just a couple of lessons
learned from going

00:35:28.785 --> 00:35:29.660
through this process.

00:35:29.660 --> 00:35:32.790
We originally used MapR
when we first built it.

00:35:32.790 --> 00:35:34.880
Native Hadoop,
migrated to Dataflow.

00:35:34.880 --> 00:35:36.070
We love Dataflow.

00:35:36.070 --> 00:35:39.900
The abstraction
level is fantastic.

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:43.410
Cannot recommend highly
enough optimizing your code.

00:35:43.410 --> 00:35:46.320
One stray logging statement
at this sort of scale

00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:49.850
equals about a five hour delay.

00:35:49.850 --> 00:35:53.610
When you're developing
your pipelines,

00:35:53.610 --> 00:35:57.380
we found it helpful to
generate multiple data sets,

00:35:57.380 --> 00:35:59.060
static data sets,
that we could then

00:35:59.060 --> 00:36:02.590
re-run both different
configurations and different

00:36:02.590 --> 00:36:04.920
logic at the same time.

00:36:04.920 --> 00:36:06.950
Sometimes the
configurations that you need

00:36:06.950 --> 00:36:10.380
vary based on your
data load, data scale.

00:36:10.380 --> 00:36:14.610
So don't assume that a small
number of large workers

00:36:14.610 --> 00:36:17.640
is better than a large number
of small workers or vice versa.

00:36:17.640 --> 00:36:19.210
It is very contingent.

00:36:19.210 --> 00:36:21.900
You will spend quite a
bit of time playing around

00:36:21.900 --> 00:36:26.860
with those to figure out
what actually suits your job.

00:36:26.860 --> 00:36:29.810
And then really learn to tune
the job to maximize the worker

00:36:29.810 --> 00:36:30.310
throughput.

00:36:30.310 --> 00:36:31.870
That, we found,
was very important.

00:36:34.670 --> 00:36:37.530
So where are we
going in the future?

00:36:37.530 --> 00:36:42.130
So the CAT bidder is supposed
to be chosen this year.

00:36:42.130 --> 00:36:45.680
Whoever wins then has one year
to get all the major exchanges

00:36:45.680 --> 00:36:49.910
on board, which is just
a silly short time frame.

00:36:49.910 --> 00:36:52.650
Year two the major broker
dealers have to come on.

00:36:52.650 --> 00:36:54.690
And year three, everybody else.

00:36:54.690 --> 00:36:59.860
So three years to build and
deploy to the entire industry.

00:36:59.860 --> 00:37:03.750
After that, things could get
interesting-- different asset

00:37:03.750 --> 00:37:06.830
classes, different
types of transactions

00:37:06.830 --> 00:37:07.870
could be brought in.

00:37:07.870 --> 00:37:10.250
Again, bigger, wider, more
holistic view of what's

00:37:10.250 --> 00:37:12.830
going on in the market.

00:37:12.830 --> 00:37:15.960
Potentially move
closer to real time.

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:19.600
Real time for the SEC for
the market at the moment

00:37:19.600 --> 00:37:21.000
is about five days.

00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:23.430
That's what they
consider real time.

00:37:23.430 --> 00:37:26.420
We can-- I mean, Dataflow
support streaming out the box.

00:37:26.420 --> 00:37:30.030
We can potentially
contract this whole process

00:37:30.030 --> 00:37:32.730
as much as the people
on the other end

00:37:32.730 --> 00:37:36.140
are able to handle that.

00:37:36.140 --> 00:37:37.880
And then we're
having discussions

00:37:37.880 --> 00:37:39.070
about opening up this.

00:37:39.070 --> 00:37:41.790
Right now, this is
a regulatory system.

00:37:41.790 --> 00:37:44.060
We think it's quite
important that the broker

00:37:44.060 --> 00:37:46.766
dealers be given access
to the data as well.

00:37:46.766 --> 00:37:48.640
We want the broker
dealers and the regulators

00:37:48.640 --> 00:37:50.590
to be having
conversations and looking

00:37:50.590 --> 00:37:52.150
at exactly the same data.

00:37:52.150 --> 00:37:56.710
The goal is to build a better
regulatory system for our stock

00:37:56.710 --> 00:37:57.990
market.

00:37:57.990 --> 00:37:59.800
And if you do that,
then the broker dealers

00:37:59.800 --> 00:38:03.610
could potentially shut off
all sorts of internal systems

00:38:03.610 --> 00:38:06.000
that they are now running
because they have to.

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:08.920
And you come to sort of
a global source of truth.

00:38:08.920 --> 00:38:12.860
It's going to save everybody
money, time, and effort.

00:38:12.860 --> 00:38:14.760
And if you take that
concept a little bit

00:38:14.760 --> 00:38:16.520
further-- I don't
do it with them

00:38:16.520 --> 00:38:19.820
very often because it scares
the living bejesus out of them--

00:38:19.820 --> 00:38:23.081
but if the data's anonymous
and if the data's secure, well,

00:38:23.081 --> 00:38:25.330
what would happen if you
opened up APIs onto that data

00:38:25.330 --> 00:38:29.390
and let the market
innovate around it?

00:38:29.390 --> 00:38:32.710
And so for us, it's exciting
because no matter who

00:38:32.710 --> 00:38:35.160
wins in this point,
all post trade data

00:38:35.160 --> 00:38:37.830
will be in the public cloud.

00:38:37.830 --> 00:38:38.840
So think about that.

00:38:38.840 --> 00:38:41.020
All the arguments that
have happened about-- it's

00:38:41.020 --> 00:38:44.990
not secure this, it's
not secure that-- done.

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:47.360
The regulators would have put
all the data in the cloud.

00:38:47.360 --> 00:38:50.290
And so hopefully that will
bring and spur the same type

00:38:50.290 --> 00:38:52.580
of innovation that we've
seen in media and internet

00:38:52.580 --> 00:38:54.670
to financial services
and eventually

00:38:54.670 --> 00:38:57.925
to other regulated industries,
such as health care, energy, et

00:38:57.925 --> 00:39:00.580
cetera.

00:39:00.580 --> 00:39:05.100
So conclusion--
scalability and security.

00:39:05.100 --> 00:39:07.030
Scalability-- no brainer.

00:39:07.030 --> 00:39:09.470
Using those technologies,
we [INAUDIBLE] go higher.

00:39:09.470 --> 00:39:14.610
Security-- again, that was a
great conversation over years.

00:39:14.610 --> 00:39:16.480
Google never had a breach.

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:17.730
Lots of breaches at the banks.

00:39:17.730 --> 00:39:21.420
I mean, the kind of conversation
solved itself there.

00:39:21.420 --> 00:39:27.130
Economic cost-- again, decreased
pricing, not a huge amount

00:39:27.130 --> 00:39:30.660
of cost off the bid, very
valuable to everybody.

00:39:30.660 --> 00:39:32.540
And the elasticity,
because of the trading

00:39:32.540 --> 00:39:36.560
daily volumes is
another key point.

00:39:36.560 --> 00:39:39.460
So the goal is to change
not just the where,

00:39:39.460 --> 00:39:41.580
but the how of
financial services

00:39:41.580 --> 00:39:43.860
computing and what can be done.

00:39:43.860 --> 00:39:46.340
This has never been done before.

00:39:46.340 --> 00:39:49.360
It's only recently it's
been technically viable.

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:52.260
It's even more recently that
it's been economically viable.

00:39:52.260 --> 00:39:54.550
So we're pretty
excited about it.

00:39:54.550 --> 00:39:56.400
However, one more
thing, which I think

00:39:56.400 --> 00:39:59.840
is the phrase that gets used
in this part of the world.

00:39:59.840 --> 00:40:03.350
We ran a test with Carter's
team two weeks ago.

00:40:03.350 --> 00:40:08.005
We process 25 billion market
events in an hour end to end.

00:40:08.005 --> 00:40:10.040
Well, technically
it was 50 minutes,

00:40:10.040 --> 00:40:13.590
but I didn't want to
be seen as showing off.

00:40:13.590 --> 00:40:16.550
That is 20% percent more than
the highest market volume

00:40:16.550 --> 00:40:18.906
day ever in the US markets.

00:40:18.906 --> 00:40:21.230
We created 25
billion FIX messages

00:40:21.230 --> 00:40:26.410
and ran exactly that
demo in 50 minutes.

00:40:26.410 --> 00:40:31.310
We used 3,500 Bigtable nodes
and 300 standard-32 VMs

00:40:31.310 --> 00:40:34.310
to do that, which
isn't that many when

00:40:34.310 --> 00:40:37.330
you consider those numbers.

00:40:37.330 --> 00:40:40.600
Sustained 22 million
events read a second.

00:40:40.600 --> 00:40:46.230
60 million events written
a second sustained.

00:40:46.230 --> 00:40:47.790
Burst-- this is
where it gets silly.

00:40:47.790 --> 00:40:50.140
It start to get
silly at this point.

00:40:50.140 --> 00:40:52.040
34 million events read a second.

00:40:52.040 --> 00:40:56.660
22 million events
written a second.

00:40:56.660 --> 00:40:58.990
CARTER PAGE: So I just
want to jump in here

00:40:58.990 --> 00:41:01.120
and provide a little context.

00:41:01.120 --> 00:41:05.120
We see a lot of traditionally
large numbers at Google.

00:41:05.120 --> 00:41:08.555
And not be cocky, but we often
kind of yawn at terabytes

00:41:08.555 --> 00:41:09.990
and things like that.

00:41:09.990 --> 00:41:13.040
These are actually
really big numbers

00:41:13.040 --> 00:41:16.690
and kind of made our team blush
being able to pull them off.

00:41:16.690 --> 00:41:21.470
To put it in context, imagine
34 million events per second

00:41:21.470 --> 00:41:24.100
is the equivalent of over
two billion events processed

00:41:24.100 --> 00:41:25.140
every minute.

00:41:25.140 --> 00:41:28.160
22 million writes per second
is over a billion events

00:41:28.160 --> 00:41:29.500
every minute.

00:41:29.500 --> 00:41:32.365
And usually when you talk about
processing billions of events,

00:41:32.365 --> 00:41:33.740
you generally are
talking about--

00:41:33.740 --> 00:41:35.926
and this is in
big data context--

00:41:35.926 --> 00:41:37.300
you get a billion
events, usually

00:41:37.300 --> 00:41:43.700
saying I did a billion events
in a day or I did it over hours.

00:41:43.700 --> 00:41:47.140
Now we're saying that
we can do billions

00:41:47.140 --> 00:41:48.630
in just a single minute.

00:41:48.630 --> 00:41:52.490
These are the actual reads and
writes to the database itself.

00:41:52.490 --> 00:41:55.830
So this is to a persistent
storage system, which actually,

00:41:55.830 --> 00:41:57.960
even though the reads
number is bigger,

00:41:57.960 --> 00:42:00.484
from an engineering
standpoint, the write

00:42:00.484 --> 00:42:01.900
is actually what
we're prouder of.

00:42:01.900 --> 00:42:03.400
And there's actually
a lot more work

00:42:03.400 --> 00:42:06.135
that goes into that because each
of those writes are durable.

00:42:06.135 --> 00:42:07.980
They're not cashed.

00:42:07.980 --> 00:42:10.870
So to put it in
another context, if we

00:42:10.870 --> 00:42:12.930
were in the middle of
this process that's

00:42:12.930 --> 00:42:15.550
going-- the task that
we did, or 50 minutes--

00:42:15.550 --> 00:42:19.280
and we're writing at an rate
of 22 million writes per second

00:42:19.280 --> 00:42:22.347
and we simultaneously yank
the plug on all the machines,

00:42:22.347 --> 00:42:23.930
every single one of
those acknowledged

00:42:23.930 --> 00:42:26.050
rights has been preserved
and will be there

00:42:26.050 --> 00:42:26.934
when it comes back.

00:42:26.934 --> 00:42:29.100
And that's something we're
actually really proud of,

00:42:29.100 --> 00:42:32.740
and I think it's a pretty
extraordinary large thing.

00:42:32.740 --> 00:42:34.670
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:39.009 --> 00:42:41.550
NEIL PALMER: So we're going to
translate that into just shear

00:42:41.550 --> 00:42:43.650
gigs per second just because.

00:42:43.650 --> 00:42:45.990
So sustained 22 gigs
reads per second,

00:42:45.990 --> 00:42:49.330
13 gigs written per second.

00:42:49.330 --> 00:42:53.560
Burst, 34 gigs read, 18
gigs written per second.

00:42:53.560 --> 00:42:55.280
It was just smoking.

00:42:55.280 --> 00:42:56.950
And so it was an utter pleasure.

00:42:56.950 --> 00:42:58.950
We've had more fun than
we know what to do with,

00:42:58.950 --> 00:43:02.900
and we're really looking forward
to taking the system forward

00:43:02.900 --> 00:43:05.390
and seeing where we go
with it and also talking

00:43:05.390 --> 00:43:07.360
about where else we can
apply this technology.

00:43:07.360 --> 00:43:09.720
So I think that's it.

00:43:09.720 --> 00:43:11.780
We'll be around for
questions if you need us.

00:43:11.780 --> 00:43:12.613
Thank you very much.

00:43:12.613 --> 00:43:13.580
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:13.580 --> 00:43:15.130
All right.

